{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>How shall one pursue</b> etc. How did the matter change, that today one of the nations pursues a thousand of ours, and <b>two</b> of them <b>put to flight ten thousand</b> of ours — but yesterday one of ours pursued a thousand of the nations, and two of ours put to flight ten thousand of theirs? And why did this happen to them? <b>Were it not that their Rock had sold them</b> — the Rock of Israel sold them." "<b>Ten thousand.</b> Ten thousand, and a proof for the matter: 'and we will take ten for a hundred, and a hundred for a thousand, and a thousand for ten thousand.'" "<b>And two put to flight ten thousand.</b> Behold, one pursues five thousand. But in the measure of good it is written: 'and five of you shall pursue a hundred, and a hundred of you shall pursue ten thousand' — behold, one pursues no more than a hundred. If so, we find the measure of punishment greater than the measure of good. One may answer: in the measure of good it is written in it 'and your enemies shall fall before you by the sword,' and therefore one need not pursue more than a hundred, for they will fall before him. But in the measure of punishment no falling is written, and the pursuer does not kill — he only pursues. Therefore it is no wonder if he pursues that many."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:55:13.368519282Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}